{"wikidata":{"entityId":"Q102626","revision":2553322915,"retrievedAt":"2026-10-15T05:06:46.593Z","labels":{"tr":"Dürbün","en":"binoculars"},"descriptions":{"en":"pair of telescopes mounted side-by-side"},"titles":{"enwiki":"Binoculars","trwiki":"Dürbün"}},"encyclopedia":{"title":"Binoculars","reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?oldid=1379942416","attribution":"Wikipedia contributors, Binoculars","license":"CC-BY-SA-4.0","termsReference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Copyrights","retrievedAt":"2026-10-15T05:08:11.875Z","extract":"Binoculars or field glasses is an optical instrument made of a pair of identical refracting telescopes mounted side-by-side towards the same direction, providing the user with magnified vision for both eyes (i.e. binocular vision). Most binoculars are sized to be held using both hands, although sizes vary widely from small, single-hand opera glasses to large pedestal-mounted tower viewers.
Unlike a monocular telescope, binoculars give users a three-dimensional image: each eyepiece presents a slightly different image to each of the viewer's eyes and the parallax allows the visual cortex to generate an impression of depth.

"}}

{"wikidata":{"entityId":"Q25308","revision":2551331129,"retrievedAt":"2026-10-15T05:06:46.593Z","labels":{"tr":"Salepgiller","en":"Orchid"},"descriptions":{"en":"family of plants","tr":"bitki familyası"},"titles":{"enwiki":"Orchid","trwiki":"Orchidaceae"}},"encyclopedia":{"title":"Orchid","reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?oldid=1374176368","attribution":"Wikipedia contributors, Orchid","license":"CC-BY-SA-4.0","termsReference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Copyrights","retrievedAt":"2026-10-15T05:08:11.875Z","extract":"Orchids are plants that belong to the family Orchidaceae (), a diverse and widespread group of flowering plants with blooms that are often colourful and fragrant. Orchids are cosmopolitan plants, living in diverse habitats on every continent except Antarctica. The world's richest diversity of orchid genera and species is in the tropics. Many species are epiphytes, living on trees. The flowers and their pollination mechanisms are highly specialized, attracting insect pollinators by colour, pattern, scent, pheromones, and sometimes by mimicking female insects. Orchids have very small seeds, relying on fungal partners for germination. Some orchids have no leaves, either photosynthesizing with their roots or relying entirely on fungal partners for food.
Orchidaceae is one of the two largest families of flowering plants. It contains about 28,000 currently accepted species in around 700 genera. That represents some 6–11% of all species of seed plants. Horticulturists run many orchid societies around the world; they have produced many hybrids and cultivars.

"}}

